1:["$","$Ld",null,{"item":{"id":"claim-and-boundary","number":"09","title":"Explain what each limitation qualifies.","mode":"Detached caveats","scale":"slide","dek":"Connect each limitation to the conclusion it affects. Explain how it changes what the audience can infer from the evidence or carry over to another setting.","before":[{"id":"carbon-result","kind":"image","title":"GDP response","image":"/originals/stock-p25.png","sourcePage":"25","alt":"GDP response to a $40 carbon-tax increase, with 67% and 95% bands over years 0–6."},{"kind":"image","title":"Closing discussion · PDF p. 40","image":"/originals/stock-p40.png","alt":"Discussion caveats: spillovers to comparison countries; endogenous tax adoption and rate changes; interaction with the EU emissions-trading system; external validity.","id":"carbon-discussion","sourcePage":"40"}],"after":[{"kind":"carbon-response","title":"Estimated GDP effects remain close to zero.","alt":"Rebuilt cumulative GDP-level response over years 0–6. Both 67% and 95% confidence bands include zero at every horizon.","revises":["carbon-result"],"subtitle":"Response to a $40 increase in the carbon tax"},{"kind":"claim","title":"The GDP response depends on the comparison and policy setting.","items":[{"title":"Causal inference","text":"Endogenous tax changes and spillovers to comparison countries may affect the estimated response."},{"title":"Policy setting","text":"The estimates describe carbon taxes operating alongside the EU emissions-trading system."},{"title":"Other settings","text":"Effects may differ with the country and the design of the tax."},{"title":"Precision","text":"The confidence bands allow both increases and decreases in GDP."}],"revises":["carbon-discussion","carbon-result"]}],"diagnosis":"The closing slide lists valid limitations after the results, but leaves listeners to connect broad terms such as endogeneity and external validity to the estimate. Pairing each concern with the inference it limits makes the closing argument easier to retain.","changes":["Restate the result with its uncertainty.","Translate each caveat into a question about that result.","Keep the original response plot available with its full specification."],"preserved":"All four source concerns: spillovers, endogenous tax changes, interaction with emissions trading, and external validity. The original GDP-level plot and both confidence bands are retained.","deferred":"Other outcomes and specifications remain in the original deck. A near-zero estimate does not establish that the true effect is exactly zero.","practice":"Write “What this establishes” and “What it leaves open” in equally plain language. Check both with your advisor.","principle":"Be bigger than your paper","sourceType":"public","context":"FRBSF seminar. Results occupy PDF pp. 21–38 and are summarized on p. 39; p. 40 is the closing caveat slide. This local repair links its concerns to the cumulative GDP-level response on p. 25.","beforeCaption":"","afterCaption":"The GDP response, followed by its interpretation.","source":{"authors":"Gilbert E. Metcalf, James H. Stock","title":"The Macroeconomic Impact of Europe’s Carbon Taxes","date":"16 July 2020","url":"https://www.frbsf.org/economic-research/wp-content/uploads/sites/4/Stock-FRBSF-Seminar-071620.pdf","pages":"25, 40"},"values":["The GDP-level response is replotted from the chart on PDF p. 25. Coordinates were digitized from its raster plot and are approximate.","The plot retains horizons 0–6, both 67% and 95% bands, and the zero line. The vertical axis is tightened to ±3 percentage points so the bands can be read.","The experiment is a $40 carbon-tax increase in real 2018 US dollars, weighted by coverage share. Sample EU+; restricted local projections; parallel-path assumption; four lags.","Approximate point estimates at years 0–6: 0.04, 0.13, 0.31, 0.29, −0.04, 0.08, 0.08 percentage points."],"topic":"Climate & macro","point":"A limitation is informative when the audience understands its consequence for a claim. State the finding, identify the inference at issue, and explain how a remaining uncertainty or assumption restricts that inference. Distinguish questions about causal interpretation, precision, and applicability to other settings, so listeners understand both what the work establishes and what remains open."}}]
